Venetium 0068 - feature level 9_x shader limits: no "skip validation" retry in ANGLE's D3D11 backend, a safe failed draw, no Skia path tessellation.
1. With 0067 the Lumia 735 (Adreno 305, qcdx9um8974 9.14.1140, ANGLE D3D11 at vs_4_0_level_9_3) gets past its first
   frame, but 11-17 s later the GPU process exits with RESULT_CODE_GPU_EXIT_ON_CONTEXT_LOST, three times, and Chromium
   turns the GPU off. Just before each exit ANGLE logs one "C:\fakepath(136,14): error X5615: Compiled shader code
   uses too many instruction slots (466). Max. allowed by the target (vs_2_x) is 256." At 9_x the D3D11 backend
   compiles vs_4_0_level_9_3, whose level-9 part is vs_2_x: 256 instruction slots. Upstream ANGLE never creates a
   D3D11 device below 10_0 (0045 adds 9_3 on ARM32), so neither ANGLE nor Skia has a rule for it.
2. Reproduced on the server with the x86 ANGLE forced to WARP at 9_3 (a test-only hook, not in this patch): the same
   error at the same place, (136,14), 467 slots. The HLSL is Skia's stroke tessellation shader (tessControlArgs =
   radial segments per radian, join type, stroke radius; per-instance curve points), drawn by
   TessellationPathRenderer / StrokeTessellateOp: 467 slots optimized, 988 with skip optimization, 538 with avoid
   flow control, so it can never compile at 9_x.
3. ANGLE compile: Renderer11::compileToExecutable retries a failed compile with "skip validation", "skip
   optimization" and "avoid flow control". Validation is the step that holds a level-9 shader to the 9_x limits, so
   at 9_x the "skip validation" retry compiles the over-limit shader (checked with D3DCompile on WARP: a shader that
   fails "default" with X5615 at 402 slots compiles with D3DCOMPILE_SKIP_VALIDATION, 402 slots, and the device takes
   it). That is the single X5615 per GPU process in the 735's log; the context loss follows. At 9_x there is no "skip
   validation" retry now. The D3D backend still links a program whose executable failed (link status true, the
   compiler message in the info log); each draw that needs the executable fails instead, whichever program it is
   (Skia's, the compositor's or a WebGL page's).
4. ANGLE draw path: that makes a failed draw routine at 9_x, which exposed two holes in it (found in review,
   reproduced on WARP at 9_3 with a WebGL page that draws an over-limit vertex shader again and again with the same
   program and buffers, changing only a uniform). StateManager11::updateState resets each dirty bit before it syncs
   it, so after a failed program sync the next draw skipped the program and built an input layout from the missing
   vertex executable: the second draw crashed the GPU process (STATUS_ACCESS_VIOLATION). The shaders now stay dirty
   after a failed sync, and InputLayoutCache fails the draw rather than dereference a null executable. And every
   failed draw logged an ERR line plus a GL error (which ANGLE logs again), while such a program is drawn every
   frame: the first 8 per GPU process are reported, the rest fail quietly, as the D3D9 path treats a failed program
   (0032/0053). With this, on WARP at 9_3: 20 draws, 8 GL errors, no crash, the context kept. These three only run
   when an executable is missing, which is routine at 9_x and rare elsewhere.
5. Skia: GrGLCaps already turns the tessellation path renderer off on ANGLE's D3D9 backend (the Tegra: "D3D9 conic
   strokes fail") and on native Adreno 3xx. At 9_3 it stayed on, because the backend is D3D11 and instancing is
   there. It is now also off when the ANGLE renderer string says "_level_9_" ("Direct3D11 vs_4_0_level_9_3 ..."), so
   strokes and fills take the other path renderers, as on the Tegra. The tessellator only ever took non-antialiased
   draws (coverage AA never used it), so that is all this changes. On WARP at 9_3, a page of crisp-edge strokes,
   dashes, concave even-odd and nonzero fills and clip paths draws none of its strokes while the tessellator is on,
   and draws the same as at full feature level with it off and stencil on (checked with instanced arrays turned off,
   which takes the tessellator out too): no X5615, no failed draw, no depth-stencil error.
   The compile change and the Skia rule change behavior only at feature level 9_x, which only ARM32 builds create
   (0045), so neither is _M_ARM-only.

diff --git a/third_party/angle/src/libANGLE/renderer/d3d/d3d11/Context11.cpp b/third_party/angle/src/libANGLE/renderer/d3d/d3d11/Context11.cpp
--- a/third_party/angle/src/libANGLE/renderer/d3d/d3d11/Context11.cpp
+++ b/third_party/angle/src/libANGLE/renderer/d3d/d3d11/Context11.cpp
@@ -13,6 +13,7 @@
 
 #include "libANGLE/renderer/d3d/d3d11/Context11.h"
 
+#include <atomic>
 #include <utility>
 
 #include "common/entry_points_enum_autogen.h"
@@ -46,6 +47,19 @@
 
 namespace
 {
+// Venetium 0068: an executable that failed to compile (at feature level 9_x: over the shader-model limits) fails every
+// draw that needs it, and a program like that is usually drawn again every frame. Report the first few (an ERR line
+// plus a GL error, which ANGLE logs too) and fail the rest quietly, as the D3D9 path treats a failed program
+// (0032/0053). The info log can be empty: a signature that already failed, at link time or on an earlier draw,
+// returns no executable without compiling again (0053).
+bool ReportFailedDrawExecutable()
+{
+    constexpr unsigned int kMaxReports = 8;
+    static std::atomic<unsigned int> sReported{0};
+    return sReported.load(std::memory_order_relaxed) < kMaxReports &&
+           sReported.fetch_add(1, std::memory_order_relaxed) < kMaxReports;
+}
+
 ANGLE_INLINE bool DrawCallHasDynamicAttribs(const gl::Context *context)
 {
     VertexArray11 *vertexArray11 = GetImplAs<VertexArray11>(context->getState().getVertexArray());
@@ -996,9 +1010,13 @@
                                                                          &vertexExe, &infoLog));
         if (!executableD3D->hasVertexExecutableForCachedInputLayout())
         {
-            ASSERT(infoLog.getLength() > 0);
-            ERR() << "Error compiling dynamic vertex executable: " << infoLog.str();
-            ANGLE_TRY_HR(this, E_FAIL, "Error compiling dynamic vertex executable");
+            // Venetium 0068: see ReportFailedDrawExecutable.
+            if (ReportFailedDrawExecutable())
+            {
+                ERR() << "Error compiling dynamic vertex executable: " << infoLog.str();
+                ANGLE_TRY_HR(this, E_FAIL, "Error compiling dynamic vertex executable");
+            }
+            return angle::Result::Stop;
         }
     }
 
@@ -1023,9 +1041,13 @@
                                                                          &infoLog));
         if (!executableD3D->hasPixelExecutableForCachedOutputLayout())
         {
-            ASSERT(infoLog.getLength() > 0);
-            ERR() << "Error compiling dynamic pixel executable: " << infoLog.str();
-            ANGLE_TRY_HR(this, E_FAIL, "Error compiling dynamic pixel executable");
+            // Venetium 0068: see ReportFailedDrawExecutable.
+            if (ReportFailedDrawExecutable())
+            {
+                ERR() << "Error compiling dynamic pixel executable: " << infoLog.str();
+                ANGLE_TRY_HR(this, E_FAIL, "Error compiling dynamic pixel executable");
+            }
+            return angle::Result::Stop;
         }
     }
 
